  "Number": 49,
  "Question": "The graphs of \\(y=\\dfrac{x^2-4}{x-2}\\) and \\(y=2x\\) intersect in:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "one point whose abscissa is \\(2\\)",
    "B": "one point whose abscissa is \\(0\\)",
    "C": "no points",
    "D": "two distinct points",
    "E": "two identical points"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "At an intersection, both formulas must be defined and give the same \\(y\\)-value. The denominator of the first formula requires \\(x\\ne2\\). For these allowed inputs,\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2-4}{x-2}\n=\\frac{(x-2)(x+2)}{x-2}\n=x+2.\n\\]\nThus an intersection would require \\(x+2=2x\\), giving \\(x=2\\). But that input makes the original denominator zero, so it cannot give an intersection. The graphs have \\(\\boxed{\\text{no points in common, choice C}}\\).",
